From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon McGinty <brandon.mcginty@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:30:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] speakup: name Chinese and fullwidth punctuation by
 default

A speech synthesizer produces no sound for an isolated Chinese
punctuation mark, so reviewing Chinese text one character at a time was
silent at every punctuation mark.  The extended character table and the
fold mechanism are in place but empty, so nothing uses either yet.

Populate the table with 76 entries covering the code points a reader of
Chinese meets: the general punctuation at U+2013 through U+203B, the CJK
symbols and punctuation at U+3000 through U+3030, and the fullwidth and
halfwidth forms at U+FF01 through U+FF65.

The class bits mirror the class bits that `default_chartab` gives the
matching ASCII punctuation, so `punc_level` and `reading_punc` treat
Chinese punctuation exactly as they already treat Latin punctuation.

Chinese text has no spaces, so without B_WDLM nothing would end a word
and word navigation would traverse a whole line.  The marks that end a
Chinese clause or sentence therefore carry A_PUNC_WDLM, which names them
at punctuation level "all" and ends a word: U+3001, U+3002, U+FF01,
U+FF0C, U+FF1A, U+FF1B and U+FF1F.

The ten fullwidth digits U+FF10 through U+FF19 carry a fold target onto
the matching ASCII digit, so a run of fullwidth digits is spoken as one
number rather than as a sequence of digit names.

The table must stay sorted by code point, because `spk_ext_char_index`
bisects it.

A reader who wants different wording, or a different class for any of
these code points, can override both through
`/sys/accessibility/speakup/i18n/ext_characters` and
`/sys/accessibility/speakup/i18n/ext_chartab`.

Rewrite section 14.3 of `spkguide.txt`, which said that Speakup supports
Western European languages only and that support for anything else was a
work in progress.  The replacement states what now works, which device
carries whole characters, that the hardware synthesizer drivers still
carry Latin-1 only, and that a double-width character is reviewed as one
character.

Signed-off-by: Brandon McGinty <brandon.mcginty@gmail.com>
---
 Documentation/admin-guide/spkguide.txt |  23 ++++-
 drivers/accessibility/speakup/main.c   | 111 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 127 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/admin-guide/spkguide.txt b/Documentation/admin-guide/spkguide.txt
index dee6e02..408204d 100644
--- a/Documentation/admin-guide/spkguide.txt
+++ b/Documentation/admin-guide/spkguide.txt
@@ -1171,11 +1171,26 @@ administrator (or every user) will have to run the first steps until speakupconf
 save, choosing the appropriate language, in every user's home directory. Every
 user will then be able to do speakupconf load, Speakup will load his own settings.
 
-14.3.  No Support for Non-Western-European Languages
+14.3.  Languages Outside the Western European Family
 
-As of the current release, Speakup only supports Western European languages.
-Support for the extended characters used by languages outside of the Western
-European family of languages is a work in progress.
+Speakup carries the full range of Unicode characters, so a language outside
+the Western European family can be read, provided that the synthesizer in use
+can speak it.  The console must be in UTF-8 mode, and the synthesizer must be
+reached through a device that carries whole characters, which means
+/dev/softsynthu rather than /dev/softsynth.  The hardware synthesizer drivers
+still carry the first 256 characters only.
+
+A character that Speakup does not name is passed to the synthesizer as it
+stands, which is the right behaviour for a letter or an ideograph.  A
+punctuation mark is different: a synthesizer usually produces no sound for a
+punctuation mark outside its own script, so such a mark has to be named before
+it can be heard.  Speakup names the Chinese and fullwidth punctuation marks by
+default, and section 12.1 describes how to name a mark from any other script.
+
+Chinese, Japanese and Korean characters each occupy two screen columns.  The
+reading cursor treats such a character as one character rather than two, so
+moving left and right by character, and reviewing a character under the
+cursor, both behave as they do for a single-column character.
 
 15.  Using Speakup's Windowing Capability
 
diff --git a/drivers/accessibility/speakup/main.c b/drivers/accessibility/speakup/main.c
index 80fcaa8..71ecc25 100644
--- a/drivers/accessibility/speakup/main.c
+++ b/drivers/accessibility/speakup/main.c
@@ -229,6 +229,106 @@ static u_short default_chartab[256] = {
 	ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA, ALPHA	/* 248-255 */
 };
 
+/*
+ * Code points above Latin-1 that Speakup names and classifies by default.
+ *
+ * A speech synthesizer produces no sound for an isolated Chinese punctuation
+ * mark, so reviewing Chinese text one character at a time is silent unless
+ * Speakup names the punctuation mark itself.  The class bits mirror the class
+ * bits that default_chartab gives to the matching ASCII punctuation, so
+ * punc_level and reading_punc treat Chinese punctuation exactly as they treat
+ * Latin punctuation.
+ *
+ * Chinese text has no spaces, so nothing would end a word without B_WDLM on
+ * the marks that end a Chinese clause or sentence.  Those marks therefore
+ * carry A_PUNC_WDLM, which keeps them named at punctuation level "all" while
+ * also ending a word.
+ *
+ * A speech synthesizer cannot pronounce a fullwidth digit, so a fullwidth
+ * digit folds onto the matching ASCII digit.  Folding keeps a run of fullwidth
+ * digits readable as one number, which naming each digit would not.
+ *
+ * This table must stay sorted by code point.
+ */
+static const struct spk_ext_char spk_default_ext_chars[] = {
+	{ 0x2013, A_PUNC, "en dash" },
+	{ 0x2014, A_PUNC, "em dash" },
+	{ 0x2015, A_PUNC, "horizontal bar" },
+	{ 0x2018, A_PUNC, "left tick" },
+	{ 0x2019, A_PUNC, "right tick" },
+	{ 0x201c, PUNC, "left quote" },
+	{ 0x201d, PUNC, "right quote" },
+	{ 0x2026, A_PUNC, "ellipsis" },
+	{ 0x203b, PUNC, "reference mark" },
+	{ 0x3000, WDLM, "wide space" },
+	{ 0x3001, A_PUNC_WDLM, "ideographic comma" },
+	{ 0x3002, A_PUNC_WDLM, "ideographic dot" },
+	{ 0x3003, PUNC, "ditto" },
+	{ 0x3008, PUNC, "left angle" },
+	{ 0x3009, PUNC, "right angle" },
+	{ 0x300a, PUNC, "left double angle" },
+	{ 0x300b, PUNC, "right double angle" },
+	{ 0x300c, PUNC, "left corner" },
+	{ 0x300d, PUNC, "right corner" },
+	{ 0x300e, PUNC, "left white corner" },
+	{ 0x300f, PUNC, "right white corner" },
+	{ 0x3010, PUNC, "left lenticular" },
+	{ 0x3011, PUNC, "right lenticular" },
+	{ 0x3014, PUNC, "left tortoise" },
+	{ 0x3015, PUNC, "right tortoise" },
+	{ 0x3016, PUNC, "left white lenticular" },
+	{ 0x3017, PUNC, "right white lenticular" },
+	{ 0x301c, PUNC, "wave dash" },
+	{ 0x3030, PUNC, "wavy dash" },
+	{ 0xff01, A_PUNC_WDLM, "wide bang" },
+	{ 0xff02, PUNC, "wide quote" },
+	{ 0xff03, PUNC, "wide number" },
+	{ 0xff04, PUNC, "wide dollar" },
+	{ 0xff05, PUNC, "wide percent" },
+	{ 0xff06, PUNC, "wide and" },
+	{ 0xff07, A_PUNC, "wide tick" },
+	{ 0xff08, PUNC, "wide left paren" },
+	{ 0xff09, PUNC, "wide right paren" },
+	{ 0xff0a, PUNC, "wide star" },
+	{ 0xff0b, PUNC, "wide plus" },
+	{ 0xff0c, A_PUNC_WDLM, "wide comma" },
+	{ 0xff0d, A_PUNC, "wide dash" },
+	{ 0xff0e, A_PUNC, "wide dot" },
+	{ 0xff0f, PUNC, "wide slash" },
+	{ 0xff10, NUM, "zero", '0' },
+	{ 0xff11, NUM, "one", '1' },
+	{ 0xff12, NUM, "two", '2' },
+	{ 0xff13, NUM, "three", '3' },
+	{ 0xff14, NUM, "four", '4' },
+	{ 0xff15, NUM, "five", '5' },
+	{ 0xff16, NUM, "six", '6' },
+	{ 0xff17, NUM, "seven", '7' },
+	{ 0xff18, NUM, "eight", '8' },
+	{ 0xff19, NUM, "nine", '9' },
+	{ 0xff1a, A_PUNC_WDLM, "wide colon" },
+	{ 0xff1b, A_PUNC_WDLM, "wide semmy" },
+	{ 0xff1c, PUNC, "wide less" },
+	{ 0xff1d, PUNC, "wide equals" },
+	{ 0xff1e, PUNC, "wide greater" },
+	{ 0xff1f, A_PUNC_WDLM, "wide question" },
+	{ 0xff20, PUNC, "wide at" },
+	{ 0xff3b, PUNC, "wide left bracket" },
+	{ 0xff3c, PUNC, "wide backslash" },
+	{ 0xff3d, PUNC, "wide right bracket" },
+	{ 0xff3e, PUNC, "wide caret" },
+	{ 0xff3f, PUNC, "wide line" },
+	{ 0xff40, PUNC, "wide accent" },
+	{ 0xff5b, PUNC, "wide left brace" },
+	{ 0xff5c, PUNC, "wide bar" },
+	{ 0xff5d, PUNC, "wide right brace" },
+	{ 0xff5e, PUNC, "wide tilde" },
+	{ 0xff61, A_PUNC, "half width ideographic dot" },
+	{ 0xff62, PUNC, "half width left corner" },
+	{ 0xff63, PUNC, "half width right corner" },
+	{ 0xff64, A_PUNC, "half width ideographic comma" },
+	{ 0xff65, PUNC, "half width middle dot" },
+};
+
 /*
  * The extended character table describes a code point at or above 0x100, which
  * spk_chartab[] and spk_characters[] cannot describe because both are indexed
@@ -397,16 +497,21 @@ void spk_free_ext_chars(void)
  */
 int spk_reset_default_ext_chars(void)
 {
+	int count = ARRAY_SIZE(spk_default_ext_chars);
+	int size = ALIGN(count, SPK_EXT_CHARS_CHUNK);
 	struct spk_ext_char *table;
+	int i;
 
-	table = kcalloc(SPK_EXT_CHARS_CHUNK, sizeof(*table), GFP_ATOMIC);
+	table = kcalloc(size, sizeof(*table), GFP_ATOMIC);
 	if (!table)
 		return -ENOMEM;
 
 	spk_free_ext_chars();
+	for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
+		table[i] = spk_default_ext_chars[i];
 	spk_ext_chars = table;
-	spk_ext_chars_used = 0;
-	spk_ext_chars_size = SPK_EXT_CHARS_CHUNK;
+	spk_ext_chars_used = count;
+	spk_ext_chars_size = size;
 	return 0;
 }
 
